The Mushaf pages
----------------
src/data/pages/1.json through 604.json, and src/data/mushaf.json, generated
by scripts/build-mushaf.ts.

    Quran.com API v4, https://api.quran.com/api/v4
    Page layout, line numbers and QCF v2 glyph codes of the King Fahd
    Complex for the Printing of the Holy Quran's Madani Mushaf (1421H),
    and the Uthmani text of each word.

What is stored is the position of every word on its page and line, the code
of its glyph in that page's font, and its readable text. The text is the
Quran and must not be altered: src/data/mushaf.test.ts checks that all 6,236
ayat are present once each and in order. Credit: Quran Foundation, which
runs Quran.com, and the King Fahd Complex, named in the app under «عن مجلس
نور».

Quran Foundation's developer terms,
https://api-docs.quran.foundation/legal/developer-terms/, govern what is
built from its APIs, this data and the timings below among it.


The page fonts, which are not redistributed
-------------------------------------------
The 604 QCF v2 page fonts of the King Fahd Glorious Quran Printing Complex
are fetched by the browser from https://static.qurancdn.com at run time and
are not in this repository. If you ever vendor them, the Complex's terms of
use belong beside them and an entry belongs in this file.


The recitation timings
----------------------
src/data/timings/<reciter>.json and public/words/<reciter>/<surah>.json,
generated by scripts/build-timings.ts from the per-ayah and per-word
timestamps Quran.com publishes for whole-surah recordings,
https://api.qurancdn.com/api/qdc/audio/reciters/<id>/audio_files
(asked for with segments=true). What is stored, in milliseconds into each
surah's recording: where every ayah starts and ends, the stretch that
covers each page, and in public/words where each word starts and ends as
it is heard. The word segments are cleaned, not taken as they come:
malformed entries are dropped, times are rounded and kept within their
ayah, and no two words overlap. Each file names its recitation and
endpoint in its `source`.

Each timings file also records, for each surah file, its length and its
last-modified date as https://download.quranicaudio.com reported them in
answer to a HEAD request when the timings were built, so that
scripts/check-audio.ts can tell when a recording has been replaced.

The recordings themselves are streamed from https://download.quranicaudio.com
(QuranicAudio, a Quran Foundation project, which offers them free for
non-commercial use) when a reader listens to a page, a reciter seated in
the circle recites his turn, or a reciter is sampled in the list, and none
is stored here.


